  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, expect rain showers across inland northern\nWisconsin. A cold front will drop southward from the north this\nevening and tonight, bringing a period of low cloud bases and \npotentially some very light and isolated rain showers. \nNortheast winds gusting around 20 to 25 mph at the head of the\nBig Lake in the Twin Ports will slowly decrease this afternoon,\nbut they will continue to create a High Risk for rip currents \nand dangerous swimming conditions for Lake Superior beaches. \nLate tonight into Sunday, isolated lake effect rain showers are \npossible for the South Shore lake effect belts the limiting\nfactor looks to be low-level instability and lapse rates are\nfairly limited so notable lake effect rain accumulations are not\nexpected.\n\nSunday will feature an even stronger push of winds off the Big\nLake, leading to another day of High Risk for rip currents for\nthe Twin Ports beaches. A Beach Hazards Statement will again be\nneeded for Sunday and also likely again Monday. Gusts up to 35 \nmph are possible for the Twin Ports and South Shore communities \nduring the daytime so it could be somewhat windy for those \nrecreating even onshore Sunday daytime. This setup of notable \nhigh pressure centered over southeast Canada associated \nproducing the incoming cold front is bringing in a colder \nairmass, along with a much drier airmass settling over the Upper\nGreat Lakes. Northeast Minnesota will be positioned on the far \nwestern edge of this drier and seasonably cool airmass.\n\nClouds will clear from northwest to southeast Sunday night, which\nmeans a small area of frost is possible for the Minnesota\nBorderlands and interior Arrowhead by early Monday morning. Frost\nheadlines may be needed for these limited areas pending the exact\nsky cover trends over the next 24 hours. The real chill arrives\nMonday night as widespread frost is forecast for all areas, with\npockets of sub-freezing temperatures also becoming more likely by\nearly Tuesday morning. Frost headlines, and potentially Freeze\nWarnings, are likely to be needed across the region for Monday\nnight.\n\nThese frost and low-end freeze potential chances will last right\ninto Tuesday night as well as high pressure firmly controls our\nweather pattern. We will slowly begin to warm a bit by Thursday.\nExpect dry conditions through the early and middle portions of \nthe week ahead. Finally, looking toward the end of the forecast \nperiod, a potential late week system will approach, bringing \nnorth-central Minnesota some low-end 20 to 40 percent chances of\nlimited-coverage rain showers.",
